      experimentation: [80, 0.7, "d", "A"],
      // [src_santosdumont_myairships_self, src_santosdumont_linsdebarros2003, src_santosdumont_sciam_1907, src_santosdumont_sciam_1906 | 1898-1907 | self + scholar + independent journalists] On the evening of the 8 Aug 1901 Trocadero fall he gave out the specifications of No. 6, which was finished and inflated after 22 days (own account); Lins de Barros, citing contemporary reports, adds that he announced the new airship the same day. After the 18 Sep 1898 accident he flew again on 20 Sep (own account; Lins de Barros). Scientific American's Paris correspondent (14 Dec 1907) attributes No. 19's 'rapid construction in two weeks' to its simplicity and calls the speed of his switching between aeroplane and hydroplane 'truly amazing'; on 23 Oct 1906 a bolt broken in the morning was repaired and the flight made the same afternoon (Scientific American 17 Nov 1906). Several dated instances from independent and self sources; his own remark that a hurried varnish job may have caused the 8 Aug failure ('Such are the rewards of haste') shows the speed had costs, so not scored higher.
