      // [src_santosdumont_myairships_self, src_santosdumont_sciam_1901, src_santosdumont_linsdebarros2003 | 1897-1907 | self + authorized journalist interview + scholar] Several times he went ahead against the stated judgement of experienced practitioners. His balloon builders Machuron and Lachambre argued that a 113 m3 silk balloon was impossible and unstable; he had it built with longer suspension and it flew (own account; Lins de Barros describes the design). Members of the Paris aeronautical circle urged an electric motor rather than a petroleum engine under hydrogen; he kept the petroleum motor, and Heilig's 1901 interview has him say that 'from the beginning everybody was against the idea' (Scientific American digest; Lins de Barros). He dispensed with the conventional net and outer cover and in 1901 replaced rope suspensions with piano wire (own account; Lins de Barros). In 1907, after adopting the tail-first layout others followed, he reversed it, which Col. Ferber described as putting what was behind in front (Lins de Barros). A counter-instance is recorded by himself: on 18 Sep 1898 he yielded to the professional aeronauts on the starting position. strong_inference: several instances, but the objections are known mainly from his own and an authorized account.
      independent_thinking: [74, 0.6, "s", "A"],
