      // [src_santosdumont_myairships_self, src_santosdumont_linsdebarros2003, src_santosdumont_nasm_clement | 1897-1903 | self + scholar + curatorial record] When No. 1 began to fall over Bagatelle on 20 Sep 1898 he called to boys flying kites to seize the guide rope and run against the wind, which slowed the descent (own account; Lins de Barros cites it as an example of his presence of mind). On 12 Jul 1901, when a rudder cord broke near the Champ de Mars, he landed in the Trocadero gardens and repaired it from a ladder borrowed from passing workmen before completing the Eiffel Tower circuit (own account). From 1897 he adapted light motor-tricycle engines for airships, joining two tricycle cylinders in tandem on one connecting rod (own account); the NASM record confirms that No. 9 (1903) used a converted Clement motorcycle engine that also drove the envelope blower. Several instances but mostly from his own account, hence moderate confidence.
      resourcefulness: [70, 0.56, "s", "A"],
