      // [src_bdz_si1975_johnson, src_bdz_nyt_obit1956, src_bdz_tsha_cayleff | c.1931-1950 | journalists' interviews (sister, Gene Sarazen) + contemporary obituary + scholar] Several separately reported instances of practice beyond what any event required. Her sister Lillie (SI) recalls her in Dallas hitting balls until her hands were taped and refusing to stop at dark ('No, I got to hit just a few more'); SI states she often hit 1,000 balls a day in Dallas and later practised at driving ranges for up to 10 straight hours between tours; Gene Sarazen, who toured with her in 1935 (SI), said only Ben Hogan practised more and that she learned by standing ten feet away watching everything he did and then practising for hours; TSHA dates golf lessons with Stan Kertes in spring-summer 1933 between exhibition engagements; the 1956 NYT obituary independently reports up to 1,000 balls a day and taped hands, and quotes her 'As long as I am improving I will go on' (self). The NYT and Lillie accounts may share the autobiography's lineage, so this stays below 0.65.
      mastery_orientation: [74, 0.64, "s", "A"],
      // [src_bdz_si1975_johnson, src_bdz_nyt_obit1956, src_bdz_tsha_cayleff | 1930-1935 | journalists' interviews + contemporary obituary + scholar; one self letter] In a 23 Jun 1930 letter to Scurlock (self, via SI) she set herself an Olympic plan -- train the broad jump, high jump ('true Western roll'), baseball and javelin throws 'two times a week into 1932' -- and she qualified in the javelin, hurdles and high jump at the 1932 trials (TSHA, SI). The self-directed golf practice reported by her sister (SI: hitting after dark with taped hands while her sister begged to go in), by Sarazen (practising for hours after watching him) and by the NYT (1,000 balls a day) was done without coach or employer pressure. Kept moderate: the plan is self-reported, the practice evidence is also the main support for mastery_orientation, and SI records that the 1930-32 track and basketball work was organised and paid for by Employers Casualty.
      discipline: [66, 0.55, "s", "A"],
      // [src_bdz_si1975_johnson, src_bdz_tsha_cayleff, src_bdz_usga_glenn2011 | 1932-1943 | journalists + scholar + golf historian] After the 1932 AAU suspension and her turn to professional exhibitions she kept pursuing competitive golf through repeated setbacks: SI, TSHA and Glenn all document the 1935 USGA ruling, after her Texas Women's Amateur win, that she was ineligible as an amateur (the Bowens' attempt to have it rescinded failed), leaving her almost no tournaments; she toured exhibitions with Sarazen in 1935 (SI), qualified for the 1938 Los Angeles Open (TSHA, SI), then in 1940 publicly renounced professional golf and sat out the prescribed three-year period to regain amateur status, which she did in January 1943 (SI; TSHA dates reinstatement to 1943). A multi-year, multiply documented pursuit; not scored higher because the waiting period was a rule she accepted rather than a series of failures, and because her return to tournament golf after surgery in 1953-54 is deliberately not used (health exclusion).
      persistence: [70, 0.58, "s", "A"],
      // [src_bdz_si1975_johnson, src_bdz_tsha_cayleff, src_bdz_usga_glenn2011 | 1931-1952 | journalists + scholar + golf historian] Several distinct episodes of creating openings rather than waiting for them: the 1931 Scurlock letter engineering an outside offer to raise her pay (self, via SI); after turning professional in 1932-33 she called her own New York press conference and took up paid basketball, billiards, vaudeville and baseball dates (SI, TSHA), though Roy Doan and an agent booked several of them; in winter 1948, by SI's account because exhibition income 'was not enough', she and George Zaharias met Fred Corcoran and Patty Berg to found the Ladies Professional Golf Association, which TSHA (scholar) describes as created to give the handful of women professionals a tournament circuit; Dodd (SI) recounts her walking into the Rolex office to see 'the boss', which led to a golf date, a luncheon and gift watches. Several of these were joint ventures (Corcoran; Wilson's L.B. Icely funded the 1949 tour), so this is a moderate lean.
      proactive_agency: [70, 0.58, "s", "A"],
