      // [src_rontgen_glasser_1945, src_rontgen_gedaechtnisstaette | 1896-1901 | biographer + institutional memorial] Two documented decisions to give up control or reward so that the discovery and science would benefit others. When Max Levy of the AEG approached him about his firm's interest in developing the rays, he replied (as quoted by Glasser) that university discoveries 'belong to humanity and ... should not in any way be hampered by patents, licenses, contracts, nor should they be controlled by any one group'; the Würzburg Röntgen memorial independently states that he declined to patent the process. In 1901 he gave the Nobel prize money to the University of Würzburg for scientific purposes (Glasser; the memorial says the entire 50,000 kronor). Counter-signal: he did not pursue medical applications himself, offering instead to advise anyone who did (Glasser, Jan. 1896). The decisions are clear, but the motive is inferred from one quoted reply, so the score stays modest at 0.50.
